  "id": "fig-02",
  "mode": "deterministic",
  "description": "Equilibrium and released free pendulum vibration.",
  "source": {
    "image": {
      "path": "ssot-resources/soviet10year-textbooks/artifacts/10p/figures/fig-02.png",
      "sha256": "a7ebeb71a24dac73c5efa190565cb5e7c08d603489a8411d3b80cb42c003a618"
    },
    "authoritativeText": [
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c1-s2-n1",
        "text": "Oscillations are very common in nature.\nIt is easy to set an object oscillating."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c1-s2-n1",
        "text": "Hang a spring from a support and attach a small metal ball to its lower free end.\nThe spring stretches.\nAt equilibrium, the spring force $\\vec{F}_0$ balances the ball's weight $\\vec{G}$ (Figure $1$a).\n\nPull the ball gently downward, away from equilibrium, and release it.\nIt moves up and down, up and down (Figure $1$b).\nIn this motion, the body repeatedly moves first in one direction and then in the other.\n\nThis kind of motion is called oscillation.\nAfter a time, the oscillations gradually die away and the ball finally stops moving."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c1-s2-n1",
        "text": "An even simpler way to produce oscillations is to hang a small ball from a string.\nAt equilibrium, the string is vertical, and the ball's weight $\\vec{G}$ balances the string tension $\\vec{F}_0$ (Figure $2$a).\nMove the ball away from the vertical and release it: it swings back and forth (Figure $2$b) until the oscillations die away.\n\nThe ball on a string is a simple example of a pendulum①.\nMore generally, a body hung from a string or mounted on an axis so that it can oscillate under gravity is called a pendulum.\nThe axis need not pass through the body's center of gravity.\n\nA drawing ruler hung from a nail and a balance beam can both act as pendulums."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c1-s2-n1",
        "text": "① A ball on a string acts as the gravitational pendulum discussed here because gravity provides a restoring action.\nThe Earth, which produces this gravitational interaction, is included in the oscillating system that we call a simple pendulum."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c1-s2-n1",
        "text": "What is the main feature of oscillation?\nFirst, the motion repeats.\nAfter a pendulum completes one oscillation, moving from the leftmost position to the rightmost and back to the leftmost, it begins another such motion.\n\nIf the motion repeats exactly at equal time intervals, it is called periodic motion."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c1-s2-n1",
        "text": "In an oscillation, motion repeats exactly or approximately over intervals of time.\nReal oscillations need not repeat with perfect regularity."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c1-s2-n1",
        "text": "The motion of a car engine's piston, a buoy moving in waves, branches moving in the wind, and the beating of a heart are examples of repeated motion and oscillation.\nThese examples do not all have perfectly constant amplitudes or periods."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c1-s2-n1",
        "text": "**Free oscillations** In mechanics, a group of bodies whose motion we study is called a system of bodies, or simply a system.\nForces between bodies within a system are internal forces.\nForces exerted on its bodies by bodies outside the system are external forces."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c1-s2-n1",
        "text": "The simplest oscillations arise after a system is displaced from equilibrium and released, with the restoring interaction acting within the chosen system.\nThese are called free oscillations.\nA mass on a spring and a mass hanging from a string are examples.\n\nOnce displaced from equilibrium, these systems can oscillate without a periodically varying external driving force.\nCalling an oscillation free does not require real friction and air resistance to be absent."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c1-s2-n1",
        "text": "**Forced oscillations** If we move a book back and forth on a table with a hand, the book oscillates, but this is not free oscillation.\nThe motion is produced by the hand's action.\nIn the periodic motion considered here, the applied force varies periodically in magnitude and direction."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c1-s2-n1",
        "text": "Oscillations produced by a periodically varying external driving force are called forced oscillations."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c1-s2-n1",
        "text": "A piston moving back and forth in an internal-combustion engine and a sewing-machine needle moving back and forth are examples of forced oscillations."
      }
    ],
